<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace and Truce of God</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/989">989</a>–13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Pax et treuga Dei, Pax Dei, Treuga Dei, Peace of God, Truce of God</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace and Truce of God was a movement led by the Catholic Church in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> that sought, with the threat of spiritual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, to limit feuding in the western half of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>, and that drew popular support. The Peace of God, first proclaimed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-charroux">Council of Charroux</a> in 989, protected clergy, peasants, church property and other noncombatants. The Truce of God, first proclaimed at the Council of Toulouges in 1027, forbade fighting on certain days and seasons; by the middle of the 12th century about eighty days were left for fighting. The Third Lateran Council of 1179 extended the Truce to the whole Church, and the movement lasted until the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-and-truce-of-god.jpg" alt="Peace and Truce of God" width="330" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Valdavia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Revilla_de_Collazos_Church_of_San_Andrés_002_Capitel_del_arco_triunfal_con_la_Tregua_Domini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
